Third-party components
----------------------
The three JavaScript web resources shipped in dist/ are generated from the TypeScript in src/
and contain no third-party source code: every npm dependency is compile-time, type-only or
build-time, and none of them is inlined into the bundles a customer installs. The product ships
no npm package, no CLI and no node_modules directory.

  MIT:        dataverse-auth, dataverse-ify, dataverse-gen, @types/xrm, webpack, webpack-cli,
              webpack-merge, ts-loader, eslint, eslint-config-prettier, eslint-plugin-prettier,
              eslint-plugin-react, @typescript-eslint/parser,
              @typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin, prettier, vitest
  Apache-2.0: the TypeScript compiler used by the build

Those are the license fields of the 16 declared dependencies as installed, read from their own
package.json files. Both licenses permit distributing this project under Apache-2.0, and
neither adds a notice obligation for the shipped bundles.

The transitive dev-time tree (533 package.json files under node_modules/, audited 2026-10-30)
is predominantly MIT, ISC, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, 0BSD and Apache-2.0. Four entries fall
outside that group and are recorded here so that vendoring one of them, or shipping
node_modules, stays a deliberate decision rather than an accident: caniuse-lite (CC-BY-4.0, an
attribution-licensed browser-support dataset), eslint-plugin-sonarjs (LGPL-3.0), lightningcss
and its platform twin lightningcss-linux-x64-gnu (MPL-2.0), and argparse (Python-2.0). Each is
reached only through a dev dependency - linting, browser data or CSS tooling - and none of them
reaches a shipped artefact, so no additional notice is required for the web resources. Re-run
this audit if a runtime dependency is ever added.

DataStructure/Supported LCID List from Microsoft as per 28.03.2025.xlsx is reference
documentation authored by Microsoft, not a work of the authors above. It is kept in this
repository as a maintainer reference, it is NOT covered by the Apache License 2.0, and the
redistribution terms of the Microsoft source it came from were not verified: check them, or
remove or replace that file, before redistributing this repository outside the project. The
screenshots referenced from readme.md are GitHub user-attachment URLs that appear to be
project-owned captures of the user interface of this product; no third-party licensing was
found for them, and that assumption is worth re-confirming before a redistribution.

The managed solution (web resources, tables, forms, views, site map and security roles) is
assembled by hand inside a Dataverse environment from this source. Its components carry this
license and this notice as a unit with the code inside them; packaging it as a solution is not
a separate licensing decision.
